.\" dropbear(8) for Amix: Dropbear 2026.94 as this package builds it.
.\" From upstream's page by Gerrit Pape and Matt Johnston (MIT, see
.\" /opt/amix/doc/dropbear/LICENSE), rewritten in -man for the system's nroff.
.TH DROPBEAR 8 "3 November 2026" "dropbear 2026.94"
.SH NAME
dropbear \- small SSH server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /opt/amix/sbin/dropbear
[
.B \-RFEmwsgjkiaz
] [
.B \-b
.I banner
] [
.B \-r
.I hostkey
] ... [
.B \-p
[\fIaddress\fP:]\fIport\fP
] ... [
.B \-P
.I pidfile
] [
.B \-W
.I window
] [
.B \-K
.I seconds
] [
.B \-I
.I seconds
] [
.B \-T
.I tries
] [
.B \-c
.I command
] [
.B \-D
.I directory
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dropbear
is a small server for the SSH protocol, version 2.
It is started at boot by
.BR /etc/init.d/dropbear ,
which makes the host key at the first start (see
.B BOOT SCRIPT
below).
It forks into the background, listens on port 22 and starts one
process for each connection.
A user logs in with a password (checked against
.BR /etc/shadow )
or with a key listed in
.BR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .
.PP
Root may log in, by password or key, as on the console: the defaults
refuse no user, and refuse only an empty password.
.B \-w
refuses root,
.B \-g
allows root by key only; put either in
.B dropbear_flags
(below).
The server does not read
.BR /etc/default/login ,
so its
.B CONSOLE
line does not apply to it.
.PP
.B dropbear
and the client programs
.BR dbclient (1),
.BR scp (1)
and
.BR dropbearkey (1)
are one program, installed under four names.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " banner"
Show the contents of the file
.I banner
before the user logs in.
.TP
.BI \-r " hostkey"
Use the host key in the file
.IR hostkey ,
as made by
.BR dropbearkey (1).
May be given more than once, once for each key type.
Without
.BR \-r ,
the keys are read from
.B /etc/dropbear/host_ed25519
and
.BR /etc/dropbear/host_rsa ,
those that exist.
.TP
.B \-R
Make a missing host key at the first connection that needs it.
.TP
.B \-F
Stay in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-E
Log to standard error instead of
.BR syslog (3).
.TP
.B \-m
Do not show
.B /etc/motd
at login.
.TP
.B \-w
Refuse logins as root.
.TP
.B \-s
Refuse password logins (keys only).
.TP
.B \-g
Refuse password logins as root.
.TP
.B \-t
Require both a key and the password.
.TP
.B \-j
Refuse local port forwarding.
.TP
.B \-k
Refuse remote port forwarding.
.TP
\fB\-p\fP [\fIaddress\fP:]\fIport\fP
Listen on
.I port
(default 22), on every address or on
.I address
only.
Up to 10 may be given.
.TP
.B \-i
Serve one connection on standard input and output, for
.BR inetd (1M);
.B \-F
is implied and
.B \-p
is ignored.
.TP
.BI \-P " pidfile"
Write the server's process number to
.I pidfile
(default
.BR /etc/dropbear/dropbear.pid ).
.TP
.B \-a
Let other hosts connect to remotely forwarded ports.
.TP
.BI \-W " window"
The receive window of each channel, in bytes (default 24576).
A larger window can speed up a transfer and costs as much memory per
channel.
.TP
.BI \-K " seconds"
Send a keepalive after
.I seconds
without traffic (0, the default, sends none); after three unanswered
ones the connection is closed.
.TP
.BI \-I " seconds"
Close a connection idle for
.I seconds
(0, the default, never).
.TP
.BI \-M " seconds"
Close every session after
.I seconds
(0, the default, never).
.TP
.B \-z
Do not mark the traffic with the AF21 class of service.
.TP
.BI \-T " tries"
The authentication attempts allowed for one connection (default 10).
.TP
.BI \-c " command"
Run
.I command
for every login, in place of what the user asked for, which is put in
.BR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND .
.TP
.BI \-D " directory"
Where the
.B authorized_keys
files are (default
.BR ~/.ssh ).
.TP
.BI \-Q " what"
List the algorithms of one kind:
.BR kex ,
.BR sig ,
.BR cipher ,
.B mac
or
.BR compress .
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.SH ALGORITHMS
The program holds only these, in this order of preference; a client must
share one of each kind.
.TP 12
key exchange
mlkem768x25519-sha256, curve25519-sha256,
curve25519-sha256@libssh.org
.TP
host keys
ssh-ed25519, and rsa-sha2-256 with an RSA host key; the client,
.BR dbclient (1),
asks a server for rsa-sha2-256 first, which is ten times cheaper to verify
here, and then ssh-ed25519
.TP
user keys
ssh-ed25519, rsa-sha2-256
.TP
ciphers
chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com, aes128-ctr
.TP
MACs
hmac-sha2-256 (with aes128-ctr; chacha20-poly1305 has its own)
.PP
There is no finite-field Diffie-Hellman, no NIST curve, no DSA, no
SHA-1, no CBC mode, no 3DES and no compression: on a 68k each is slower
than what is offered, or weaker, or both.
The measurements are in the README of the package's source,
.B userland/packages/dropbear
in the amix repository.
.SH BOOT SCRIPT
.B /etc/init.d/dropbear
(also
.BR /etc/rc2.d/S76dropbear ,
and
.B K40dropbear
in
.B /etc/rc0.d
and
.BR /etc/rc1.d )
starts the server when the system enters run level 2 and stops it when
the system leaves it.
It reads these lines of
.BR /etc/rc.conf ;
see
.BR rc.conf (4).
.TP
.BR dropbear= yes | no
Whether the server starts (default
.BR yes ).
.TP
.BI dropbear_port= port
The port (default 22), on every address of the machine.
At boot a DHCP interface may not have its address yet, so a
.BI \-p " address" : port
in
.B dropbear_flags
fails until the lease comes.
.TP
.BR dropbear_rsa= yes | no
Also an RSA host key, for clients that know no ed25519 (default
.BR no ).
.TP
.BI dropbear_flags= options
More options for the server, such as
.BR "\-s \-g" .
.TP
.BI dropbear_keytime= seconds
How long the first start may take to make the host keys (default 900).
.PP
At its first start the script makes the ed25519 host key, and with
.B dropbear_rsa=yes
a 3072-bit RSA key, from
.BR /dev/urandom ;
making the RSA key takes minutes on a 68030.
While a key is missing, the keys are made and the server started in the
background, with
.BR rc_bg ,
so the boot goes on; the server listens once the keys are made.
A key is written under a new name and renamed when it is complete, so a
start stopped after
.B dropbear_keytime
leaves no half key, and the next start tries again.
A host key never comes from the package, so no two machines share one,
and neither an update nor
.BR pkgrm (1M)
touches it.
Without
.B /dev/urandom
(the random module) the server is not started.
What the script does is logged in
.BR /var/adm/rc.log ;
the server itself logs through
.BR syslog (3),
with the facility
.BR auth .
.PP
To run the server from
.BR inetd (1M)
instead, so that it uses no memory between connections, set
.B dropbear=no
and add to
.B /etc/inet/inetd.conf
the line
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
ssh stream tcp nowait root /opt/amix/sbin/dropbear dropbear -i -r /etc/dropbear/host_ed25519
.ft R
.fi
.PP
(and
.B ssh 22/tcp
to
.BR /etc/inet/services ,
if it is not there).
Each connection then pays the start of the program.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
The keys a user may log in with, one per line:
.RS
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
[\fIoptions\fP] ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz... \fIcomment\fP
.ft R
.fi
.PP
The file and
.B ~/.ssh
must be writable by the user only, or the keys are ignored.
The
.I options
are separated by commas:
.TP
.B no-port-forwarding
No port forwarding.
.TP
.B no-pty
No terminal.
.TP
.B restrict
Both of the above (agent and X11 forwarding are not in this build).
.TP
.BI permitopen=\(dq host : port \(dq
Local forwarding only to
.IR host : port ;
.B *
as the port allows any port.
.TP
.BI permitlisten=\(dq port \(dq
Remote forwarding only from
.IR port .
.TP
.BI command=\(dq command \(dq
Always run
.IR command ,
as
.B \-c
does.
.RE
.TP
.B /etc/dropbear/host_ed25519
.PD 0
.TP
.B /etc/dropbear/host_rsa
.PD
The host keys, readable by root only.
.TP
.B /etc/dropbear/dropbear.pid
The server's process number.
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
The switch and settings of the boot script (above).
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
What the boot script did.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rclog/dropbear
The output of a first start that made the host keys.
.TP
.B /etc/motd
Shown at login, unless
.B \-m
is given or the user has a
.BR ~/.hushlogin .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
A session gets
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.B PATH
(\fB/opt/amix/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/ucb\fP, for root
\fB/opt/amix/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/etc\fP),
.B TERM
when it has a terminal, and:
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION
The client's address and port, then the server's.
.TP
.B SSH_TTY
The terminal, when one was allocated.
.TP
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
The command the client asked for, under a forced command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dbclient (1),
.BR dropbearkey (1),
.BR scp (1),
.BR rc.conf (4),
.BR rc.subr (1M),
.BR inetd (1M)
.SH NOTES
SFTP is not served: a modern OpenSSH
.B scp
uses it by default, so give it
.B \-O
to copy with the older protocol, which
.BR scp (1)
here speaks.
.PP
Each session switches to its user when it starts the user's shell or
command, not right after the login: the 1992 system cannot keep a saved
group for the login records the way the upstream server does.
